Build "Palimpsest" — a fully offline research assistant that ships as a single executable. Every document becomes a queryable, citable knowledge base backed by an embedded graph. No Ollama. No cloud. No daemons. Works in air-gapped environments.

## THE SEVEN INNOVATIONS (why this is different)

1. TOC-AWARE CHUNKING (from TOCRAG + STAIR, 2026):
   Current retrievers chunk into length-based pieces, throwing away semantic global structure. TOCRAG extracts a document's hierarchical tree from its Table of Contents and uses it to guide chunking. STAIR achieves Recall@1 of 82.6% vs BM25's 59.5% and DPR's 68.7%. Implement this.

2. DETERMINISTIC-FIRST QUERY PLANE (from Noesis, 2026):
   On factuality-critical queries (exact values, cross-source alignment, multisource attribution), the dominant failure is confabulation, not retrieval miss. Noesis makes every deterministic judgment before generation: producer-side fact layer, positional addressing with cross-source alignment, provenance scoping. A 2B-parameter model achieves parity with a 35B model on factual integrity. Structured retrieval beats flat RAG by +11.4 points at 2B.

3. TRIPLE RELEVANCE GATE (novel — combines three 2026 papers):
   (a) Self-Calibrating Gate (maktaba, 2026): unsupervised, label-free, per-tenant relevance gate. Threshold derived from the scorer's own score geometry — in-domain self-match anchor vs cross-document out-domain anchor. F1 0.968 vs 0.828 for fixed default. Precision 0.71→0.94.
   (b) Spectral Gap Gate (Phi-377, 2026): build a bipartite retrieval graph (query↔documents), compute the second-smallest Laplacian eigenvalue λ₂. If λ₂ ≥ 0.75, accept. If < 0.75, reject or re-rank. 77% false-positive reduction.
   (c) Gap-Based Cutoff (CAR, 2026): find the largest discontinuity in the ranked score list. Score each boundary as gap_i/max_gap + i/N. Cut at the highest-scoring boundary. Accuracy 0.98 with 2.1 candidates vs Top-10's 1.00 with 10.0.
   All three gates must agree before context is used. If any gate rejects, answer from model knowledge with a clear "I don't have this in your library" disclaimer.

4. BIDIRECTIONAL GRAPH TRAVERSAL (from Noesis, 2026):
   Single-pass chunk retrieval loses cross-section semantic connections. Noesis traverses the knowledge graph bidirectionally with a Graph-Feedback Context Resolver that simulates human sequential reading with degrading memory. Produces significantly denser graphs. On HotpotQA (1,000 questions): 59.5 EM / 74.7 F1, surpassing GraphRAG by +27.8 EM.

5. CLOSED-LOOP MEMORY (from Graphonomous, 2026):
   Every query follows a five-phase loop: retrieve → route → act → learn → consolidate. Outcomes change beliefs, beliefs change retrieval. The graph gets sharper with every action. LongMemEval: 92.6% QA proxy, 98.7% session hit rate. Implement as five machines with actions (not flat tools — tool selection accuracy degrades past ~30 tools).

6. SINGLE LLAMA-SERVER, THREE MODELS (from llama.cpp, 2026):
   One llama-server instance hosts embedding, reranker, and chat models behind a single OpenAI-compatible API via a models.ini file. Reranker needs pooling=rank. Embedding needs --embedding --pooling last -c 32768.

7. NUITKA SINGLE-EXECUTABLE (from Nuitka, 2026):
   Compile to a single native binary. 2-4x runtime speedup on compute-heavy code. 20-40% smaller than PyInstaller's onefile output.

## STACK

- Inference: llama.cpp (llama-server, single binary). One process, three models.
- Embedding: Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B-Q4_K_M.gguf (385 MB, 1024-dim, ~260 emb/s on Apple Silicon MPS)
- Reranker: Qwen3-Reranker-4B-Q4_K_M.gguf (2.1 GB, ~75.2 nDCG@10) OR BGE-Reranker-v2-M3-Q4.gguf (0.34 GB, ~71.5 nDCG@10, 4x faster)
- Chat LLM: Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-Q5_K_M.gguf (~2.8 GB) — best quality/latency tradeoff per 2026 benchmarks
- Vector DB: Zvec (SQLite-like embedded, Apache 2.0, HNSW + BM25 hybrid, scalar-vector queries) OR LanceDB (fallback)
- Metadata + FTS: SQLite FTS5 (BM25 + exact phrase matching)
- Graph DB: LadybugDB (Kuzu successor, embedded Cypher, .lbug file per database, zero daemons)
- Document parsing: Kreuzberg (91+ file formats, 248 programming languages, Rust core)
- TUI: Textual
- Web: FastAPI + HTMX + Tailwind (all assets local, no CDN)
- Packaging: Nuitka --onefile
- MCP: expose as MCP server so any agent can query your library

