## PHASES

PHASE 1 — Scaffold + model setup
- pyproject.toml with uv. Python 3.11+.
- Config at ~/.palimpsest/config.toml: watch_folders, chunk_size (500), chunk_overlap_pct (15), top_k_candidates (50), top_k_rerank (8), embed_model_path, rerank_model_path, chat_model_path, relevance_gate_enabled (true), spectral_gate_threshold (0.75), bidirectional_traversal_depth (2), deterministic_first_enabled (true), memory_loop_enabled (true).
- models.ini: three model entries (embedding with --embedding --pooling last -c 32768; reranker with --reranking --pooling rank; chat with default settings). Single llama-server instance loads all three.
- setup_models.py: download Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B-Q4_K_M.gguf, Qwen3-Reranker-4B-Q4_K_M.gguf, Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-Q5_K_M.gguf. Verify SHA256. Print next steps.

PHASE 2 — Ingestion
- parser.py: Kreuzberg wrapper. Handle PDF, DOCX, XLSX, PPTX, EPUB, MD, TXT, HTML, code files (248 programming languages), and images (OCR). Extract per-page text, tables, and metadata.
- toc.py: for PDFs and EPUBs, extract the internal TOC. Build a hierarchical tree: chapter → section → subsection → page range.
- chunker.py: if TOC exists, split on chapter boundaries first. Within each chapter, split on headings, then paragraphs, then sentences. Target 500 tokens, 15% overlap. Every chunk carries: chunk_id, doc_id, page_number, char_start, char_end, heading_path (e.g. "Ch 2 > Sec 3 > ¶12"), toc_depth.
- embedder.py: POST to llama-server /v1/embeddings with model=embedding. Batch 32. Handle failures with exponential backoff.
- watcher.py: watchdog on configured folders. Debounce 2s. Hash file to skip unchanged. On re-ingest, hash each chunk and only re-embed changed chunks.

PHASE 3 — Storage
- vectors.py: Zvec table `chunks` with columns: chunk_id, doc_id, vector (1024-dim), text, page, heading_path. Create HNSW index. Create BM25 FTS index on text.
- fts.py: SQLite FTS5 virtual table for BM25 + exact phrase matching.
- metadata.py: SQLite tables documents(doc_id, source_path, title, author, page_count, toc_json, file_hash, ingested_at), chunks(chunk_id, doc_id, page, heading_path, text_hash, toc_depth), sessions, messages.
- graph.py: LadybugDB. Nodes Entity{id, name, type, confidence}, Document{id, title}, Chunk{id, doc_id}. Edges MENTIONS(Chunk→Entity, count), RELATES(Entity→Entity, predicate, confidence, source_chunk), PART_OF(Chunk→Document).

PHASE 4 — Retrieval with Triple Gate
- deterministic.py: FTS5 exact phrase match first. If a query contains quoted strings or exact identifiers (dates, IDs, proper nouns), try exact match before any probabilistic retrieval. Return structured facts if found.
- hybrid.py: run BM25 (FTS5) and vector search (Zvec HNSW) in parallel. Fuse with RRF at k=60. Return top 50 candidates.
- rerank.py: POST to llama-server /v1/rerank with model=reranker. Rerank 50 → 8.
- gate_calibrating.py: maktaba-style. Compute in-domain self-match anchor (highest score in the candidate set) and cross-document out-domain anchor (median score of an unrelated control set). Derive threshold from their ratio. Accept if top score exceeds threshold. No training, no labels, no user feedback.
- gate_spectral.py: build bipartite graph (query ↔ reranked candidates). Compute Laplacian L = D - W. Find λ₂ (second-smallest eigenvalue via Lanczos, ~45ms for 10K nodes). If λ₂ ≥ 0.75, accept. If < 0.75, reject or re-rank.
- gate_gap.py: CAR largest-gap cutoff. For each boundary i in the ranked list, score_i = gap_i / max_gap + i/N. Cut at argmax(score_i). Include all candidates above the cut.
- Graph walk (PHASE 5, but called here if enabled): for each surviving chunk, extract its entity IDs from LadybugDB. Walk edges bidirectionally up to depth 2. Pull related chunks. Add to candidate set.
- hydrate.py: two-tier context. Tier 1: skeleton (headings, entity names, fact summaries) at ~30% prompt size. Tier 2: on model request, hydrate with verbatim text. Noesis reports hydration recovers verbatim narrative in ~8s vs ~29s for eager context.

PHASE 5 — Bidirectional Graph Traversal (Innovation 4)
- graph_walk.py: for each reranked chunk, extract entity IDs from the graph. Walk MENTIONS edges backward (chunk → entity) and RELATES edges forward (entity → related entities) and backward (entity ← related entities). Simulate degrading memory: weight each hop by a decay factor (0.7^depth). Collect related chunks. Re-score and merge with the original candidate set.
- Noesis reports this produces significantly denser graphs than single-pass baselines. 90% precision on extracted long-range causal edges on a 193-page document.

PHASE 6 — Synthesis (Innovation 2)
- llm.py: async client for llama-server /v1/chat/completions. Default model: Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-Q5_K_M. Streaming enabled.
- prompt.py: system prompt enforces four hard rules:
  1. Answer ONLY from provided context chunks.
  2. Every factual claim ends with [n] citing the source chunk.
  3. If context is insufficient, say so explicitly — do not guess.
  4. If the triple gate rejected, answer from model knowledge but prefix with "This is not from your library:".
- citation.py: parse [n] markers, map to chunk_ids, render as (Title, p. 42, §2.3) with clickable links. Streaming citations render progressively.
- Refusal gate: if all three gates reject AND the model's own confidence is low, refuse. This is a quality feature, not a limitation.

PHASE 7 — Closed-Loop Memory (Innovation 5)
- loop.py: five phases per query:
  1. retrieve — what do I know?
  2. route — what should I do?
  3. act — do it
  4. learn — did it work?
  5. consolidate — clean up
- belief.py: AGM-rational belief revision. Confidence updates per entity/relation. When a query succeeds, boost the confidence of chunks/entities that contributed. When it fails, demote.
- frontier.py: Wilson score epistemic frontier. Track which topics the system knows well vs poorly. Surface gaps as "I don't have this."
- Every query writes back to the graph: which chunks helped, which entities were central, which strategy worked. Next session starts from what was already established.

PHASE 8 — Interfaces
- CLI (Textual):
  - palimpsest ask "..." — one-shot
  - palimpsest chat — interactive with streaming, source panel
  - palimpsest ingest <path>
  - palimpsest watch
  - palimpsest graph <entity> — explore graph
  - palimpsest stats — docs, chunks, entities, index size
  - palimpsest models — show loaded models, VRAM usage
- Web (FastAPI + HTMX):
  - /chat with SSE streaming
  - Source panel: hover a citation to see the chunk text
  - Document browser
  - Graph explorer (pyvis, local only)
  - All assets local — no CDN, no external fonts
- MCP server:
  - search_knowledge(query, budget) — returns cited subgraph
  - get_context(chunk_ids) — hydrate with verbatim text
  - graph_query(entity) — explore connections
  - Any MCP client (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex) can query your library.

PHASE 9 — Packaging (Innovation 7)
- build.py: Nuitka --onefile --standalone. Bundle all Python dependencies. Models are downloaded separately (too large to bundle). The executable finds models in ~/.palimpsest/models/.
- Startup sequence: check models exist, start llama-server as a child process, wait for health check, start the CLI/web/MCP interface.
- Shutdown: terminate llama-server cleanly.

PHASE 10 — Tests + docs
- test_ingest: parse a sample PDF via Kreuzberg, verify TOC extraction and chunk boundaries.
- test_embed: verify llama-server /v1/embeddings returns 1024-dim vectors.
- test_hybrid: verify RRF at k=60 fuses BM25 and vector results.
- test_rerank: verify reranker reorders candidates.
- test_gate_calibrating: verify self-calibrating threshold adapts to corpus.
- test_gate_spectral: verify λ₂ gate rejects fragmented retrievals.
- test_gate_gap: verify gap-based cutoff selects the right boundary.
- test_graph_walk: verify bidirectional traversal pulls related chunks.
- test_deterministic: verify exact phrase match works before embedding.
- test_memory_loop: verify beliefs update after a query.
- test_refusal: verify all-gates-reject triggers refusal.

## CONSTRAINTS

- Zero network calls at runtime after setup. No telemetry. No cloud. No Ollama.
- All models are GGUF files loaded via llama-server.
- Config at ~/.palimpsest/config.toml
- Cross-platform: Windows, macOS, Linux
- All tests must run offline

## WHAT NOT TO DO

- Do NOT use Ollama. Use llama-server directly.
- Do NOT use nomic-embed-text. Use Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B (1024-dim).
- Do NOT build separate BM25. Use SQLite FTS5 or Zvec's native hybrid.
- Do NOT use a single relevance threshold. Use the triple gate.
- Do NOT skip TOC extraction. It's the highest-value single improvement.
- Do NOT use flat vector search. Use hybrid + rerank + graph walk.

